%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: ad496817cc2ac7b498b32f0fc2de6fb3, type: 3}
  m_Name: R1_02_WeightOfThings
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::JournalEntry
  title: "2 \xB7 Weight of Things"
  pages:
  - bigIdea: "Mass is how much stuff something has. Weight is how hard gravity pulls
      on it: W = m\xB7g."
    notes: This hall was a testing room. Three crates, the same size, no markings.
      The three pedestals by the gate only light up when they get the weight they
      want, and they ignore people. The old test scale by the door still reads any
      weight. Size tells you nothing here. Mass does.
    lookCloser: "W = m \xD7 29.43 m/s\xB2\n1 kg \u2192 <color=#8a5a2e>29.4 N</color>    
      2 kg \u2192 <color=#8a5a2e>58.9 N</color>     3 kg \u2192 <color=#8a5a2e>88.3
      N</color>\nThe three scales need 25 N, 55 N and 85 N."
    funFact: On the Moon your mass is exactly the same. Your weight is only about
      1/6.
    marginP1: They all look the same to me.
    marginP2: Then let's weigh them.
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Crate

        ? kg'
      forces:
      - label: Support
        newtons: 58.9
        direction: 2
        kind: 4
        unit: N
      - label: Weight
        newtons: 58.9
        direction: 3
        kind: 3
        unit: N
      caption: On a scale, the support force equals the weight. That's what it reads.
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  - bigIdea: "The same force moves a light object more: a = net force \xF7 m."
    notes: "A quick test without a scale: push it. Friction grips each crate with
      \u03BC\xB7m\xB7g, so heavier crates grip harder. If one of you can move it
      alone, it's the light one."
    lookCloser: "Friction \u03BC\xB7m\xB7g:  1 kg <color=#7d3029>9.8 N</color>  
      2 kg <color=#7d3029>19.6 N</color>   3 kg <color=#7d3029>29.4 N</color>\nOne
      push <color=#a26029>12 N</color>: moves only 1 kg  (a = 2.2 \xF7 1 = 2.2 m/s\xB2)\nTwo
      pushes <color=#a26029>24 N</color>: moves 2 kg too  (a = 4.4 \xF7 2 = 2.2 m/s\xB2).
      3 kg: no."
    funFact: 'Newton''s second law fits in three letters: F = ma.'
    marginP1: If it won't budge, it's my turn.
    marginP2: My tether pulls 24 N on its own.
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Crate

        2 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Two pushes
        newtons: 24
        direction: 0
        kind: 0
        unit: N
      - label: Friction
        newtons: 19.6
        direction: 1
        kind: 2
        unit: N
      caption: "Net force 4.4 N, so a = 4.4 \xF7 2 = 2.2 m/s\xB2."
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  - bigIdea: "One burst, different speeds: \u0394v = J \xF7 m. Lighter crates go
      faster and slide further."
    notes: "Careful near the acid. A burst that nudges the heavy crate sends the
      light one flying across the hall. Friction slows every sliding crate at the
      same rate, \u03BC\xB7g \u2248 9.8 m/s\xB2, so speed decides the distance. Count
      your bursts."
    lookCloser: "\u0394v = <color=#896923>15 N\xB7s</color> \xF7 m:   3 kg \u2192
      5 m/s     1 kg \u2192 15 m/s\nSlide on flat ground:  d = v\xB2 \xF7 (2\xB7\u03BC\xB7g)
      = v\xB2 \xF7 19.6\n5 m/s \u2192 about 1.3 m      15 m/s \u2192 about 11.5 m"
    funFact: 'Mass doesn''t change how fast friction slows a slide: heavy and light
      crates both lose 9.8 m/s every second.'
    marginP1: Gentle is not my style.
    marginP2: Then I'll fetch the small one.
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Crate

        3 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Burst
        newtons: 15
        direction: 0
        kind: 5
        unit: "N\xB7s"
      caption: "\u0394v = 15 \xF7 3 = 5 m/s, then it slides about 1.3 m."
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  tip: 'Not sure which crate is which? Weigh it on the test scale by the door, or
    push it: if one of you can move it alone, it''s the light one. The light crate
    waits in the niche above the door; the Tether can reach it from the high step.'
  sketch: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 8613d9d4edb29cf4093ff742569ff274, type: 3}
  sketchCaption: 'ALL: every arrow at once.'
